"Robert (Bob) McGwier" <rwmcgwier@home.com> writes:

> Both the SX164 and LX164 are crippled by the 66 Mhz bus.

Despite this, they have *excellent* main memory bandwidth. What's your
problem?

Check out the stream main memory benchmark at:

http://www.cs.virginia.edu/stream/

Machine ID                ncpus    COPY    SCALE      ADD    TRIAD
------------------------------------------------------------------
DEC_164LX_533_2MBL3          1    241.5    234.4    267.4    256.0
DEC_164SX_533_2MBL3          1    273.1    256.0    292.6    292.6

Generic_440BX_400            1    304.0    307.9    363.3    315.4
Generic_440BX_350            1    279.3    279.3    329.1    271.1

These Intel boxes use a 100mhz bus, so you can see that the Compaq
machines get much higher performance than you would expect; they use a
wider path.

Compaq's new machines still don't have a 100mhz main memory, and look
what they do:

Compaq_AlphaServer_DS10      1    881.1    929.9    839.9    900.9
Compaq_AlphaServer_DS20      1   1077.0   1244.0   1179.0   1323.0
Compaq_AlphaServer_ES40      1   1004.6   1193.9   1058.6   1237.1
Compaq_XP1000                1    900.2    971.2    917.3    967.7

Don't fall into the trap of thinking that the mhz of your main memory
is the only factor that affects performance.

-- g

[ Note: the new Compaqs need Compaq's C compiler to achieve these
results. ]




