Hey, I'm making progress!! I finally figured out some things about
these VME boards. What I've done is hit the old digital.com homepage
and found the detailed specs on the board. After referencing the Alpha
HOWTO I've discovered the architecture is what they call an eb64+ /
Cabriolet. If you can find the exact model of CPU on your VME you can
match it to a more standard model to determine what architecture will
run on it.

Next, I set up a Linux box to do bootp since the CD I have from Debian
is designed for systems with heads and my box doesn't have video /
keyboard / mouse. I have been playing with binary MILO and linux
kernels I've found on the Internet, but all of them are either tied to
VGA consoles or the wrong architecture.

What I am looking for now is a person running an eb64+ or Cabriolet who
can compile either the MILO firmware or a kernel I can boot over bootp
that directs the linux console to the serial console on the card. I
have actually gotten MILO to boot on the system using bootp, but it
fails when it can't detect the VGA card and keyboard/mouse. Here are
the most helpful links I've found for learning all this:

http://www.digital.com/oem/products/vmesbc/vmesbc.htm
