On Fri, 27 Aug 1999 20:50:26 GMT, Scott Burns
<scott4000@technologist.com> wrote: 
>    I know Alpha systems make great HTTP (web) servers, but the
>processor on the Multia is a very old model of the Alpha processor.
>Will a 166Mhz Multia system with 32MB of true parity ram outperform a
>100Mhz Pentium with the same amount of 90ns SIMMS?  If so, by how much?

That depends on whether your application is FP-bound, I/O-bound, or
memory-bound.

- If it is I/O-bound, then the speed of the CPU is irrelevant in
  comparison with the speed of the disk drive.  The built-in disk
  isn't real impressive, so the Pentium box will likely win.

- If it is memory-bound, then the fact that the Multia is starved for
  cache will hurt it, and the Pentium box will probably win.

- If it is bound by FP operations, then the fact that Alpha does FP
  better than IA-32 should allow the Multia to "win."

Your Milage Will Vary.
-- 
