There are two ways of significantly improving the speed of the Seti client
on your Alpha.

1) Link the Linux client with cpml.  To do this proceed as follows:

Get cpml from 'http://www.unix.digital.com/linux/cpml.htm'.
		mv cpml.0.1.tar.gz /usr/src/
                cd /usr/src/
                tar -xzvf cpml.0.1.tar.gz
                cd cpml.0.1/
                less README
                ar -x libcpml.a
                ld -shared -o libcpml.so *.o
                strip libcpml.so
                mv /lib/libm-2.0.7.so /lib/original_libm-2.0.7.so
                mv libcpml.so /bin/libm-2.0.7.so
                mv cpml.h /usr/include/
This scheme was developed by Paavo Hartikainen <pahartik@sci.fi>

Unfortunately, libcpml is not full compatible with libm.  If you do
all of the steps above you will find that programs like gimp no
longer work.

So, skip the last 3 steps and put libcpml.so somewhere sensible
(eg /usr/local/libcpml/libm.so).  Then run your client as follows:
setenv LD_LIBRARY_PATH /usr/local/libcpml; setiathome

If you do an strace you should see it linking to the cpml library.

This increased the speed on my 533 MHz 21164a from 11:15 to 9:15

2) Get the libraries required to run DU binaries under Linux.  These
used to be propriatory, but did I read recently that Compaq had an
agreement to let SuSE distribute them with their Alpha version?

Anyway, if you have the libraries you follow the instructions at
www.alphalinux.org for running the DU netscape (which works fine BTW).
Finally, get the alpha-dec-osf3.0 client and run it.

This increased my speed to 7:40.  The one disadvantage is that your
results are not then contributing to the Alpha Linux total (since
the seti servers think that you are running OSF).

3) The observant amongst you will have noticed that some new Alpha
clients have recently appeared on the seti results pages with names
like alpha-compaq-T64Uv4.0d.  Since there are specially optimised ones
for EV6 and EV67 machines, I presume that the standard one is being run
primarily on EV56 systems, yet it still achieves an average WU time of
2:49 WOW!!!!!!!!  These clients appear to be for Tru64 and from
discussions on comp.os.vms I deduce that they have been compiled by
Compaq staff in their spare time.  There seems to be a reasonable chance
that these clients would work on Linux using the DU binaries trick
outlined in section 2 above.  I understand that the huge speed gains
have primarily been achieved by using the hand tuned FFT algorithms from
the dxml library (a Linux version of which has recently been released as
cxml).

The big problem is that there appears to be no way in which Alpha users
outside of Compaq can get hold of these clients.  Since the clients
have processed more than enough work units to be certain that they
function correctly, that leaves two possible reasons for their
non-availability:

a) Having compiled the code the Compaq engineers are using them to
advance their own position in the results tables and do not want any
competition from their customers.

b) Compaq have sent the new clients to the seti folk who can't be bother
to put them on the download page.  Since this page hasn't changed since
the 30th July and I can't believe that none of the other Unix versions
have been updated in that time this seems quite a likely explanation.

Can I encourage Alpha Linux users to barrage the seti folk with requests
for these clients to be made available.

I hope that this somewhat long explanation helps.

