Steve <certus@home.com> wrote in message news:37C2423B.2FAB1B7E@home.com...
> I'm having some trouble installing redhat 6.0 on my  XL266.
> I have made all the boot disks stated by the documentation:
>     a MILO disk    with the xl.img
>     a boot disk       with the generic.img
>     a ramdisk disk   with the ramdisk.img
>
> I have set-up the alpha firmware just as the documentation states with a
> boot MILO option in the boot menu.
>
> I boot MILO everything goes great, MILO boots no problem from the
> floppy, I'm at the MILO prompt, the documentation tells me to type "boot
> floppy"
> NOTHING happens, except an error:
> MILO: unknown filesystem on device void
> MILO: Failed to load the kernel
>
> HELP.....I'm new to linux and need some help....I've tried "boot fd0"
> and virtually all other combinations I can think of.
>
> cheers
>
After starting MILO, insert the boot floppy and type:

ls fd0

This produces a listing of the root directory of the floppy. Notice the
kernel image. It is called something vmlinux or vmlinuz, possibly
vmlinux.gz or vmlinuz.gz or similar. To boot Linux, type (still with boot
floppy inserted):

boot fd0:<image> root=/dev/fd0 ramdisk=1

(where <image> is the name of the kernel image you just determined)
This command instructs MILO to boot the named kernel image from
floppy, use /dev/fd0 (floppy) as root device, but load it into ramdisk.
Change the boot floppy for the root floppy when instructed to do so.

Later, when you completed the Linux installation, you will boot your
Linux installation with a command like:

boot sda2:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda2

This is an example; it assumes that you have a root filesystem on
disk partition sda2 (second partition of first SCSI-disk) and that
the boot image vmlinux.gz can be found in the root directory of that
partition. You must replace these items with your actual situation.

To avoid having to type the above command every time you want to
boot your Linux system, enter the above boot command (without
the "boot" keyword) in the boot option field of the MILO boot item.
MILO will then automatically boot Linux.

Good luck,

