Sean's mail:

Here is the wrapper program I wrote to assist with the debugging of
unaligned traps.  Its the bastard child of something similar Richard
Henderson posted here, and the execcap program that Andrew Morgan wrote
for the libcap package, with a few bits of mine thrown in. Thanks to
Michal Jaegermann for the pointers. Basically, it runs the program given
on the commandline in gdb with the given arguments, but sets the flags so
that the program will recieve a SIGBUS on encountering an unaligned trap.
What you do is compile the code below thussly... gcc unaligned.c -o
unaligned ...then you run your naughty program.  Say I want to debug the
horrid "ping" program that came with redhat 6 (yes I know there's an
update, this is just an example).  What you do is get the source, build it
(without stripping the debugging symbols), change to the directory so that
gdb can find the relevant source files (in this case the directory is
called "ping"), and go unaligned ping harold ... the "harold" is because
I'm testing it by pinging one of the hosts on my network here.  You'd
substitute a suitable alternative. You'll see the following.... Extra
arguments passed to gdb in file /tmp/file6krPqZ. Be sure to delete it when
you're done.

GNU gdb 4.17.0.11 with Linux support
Copyright 1998 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
GDB is free software, covered by the GNU General
Public License, and you are welcome to change it and/or distribute copies
of it under certain conditions.
Type "show copying" to see the conditions.
There is absolutely no warranty for GDB.  Type "show warranty" for
details.
This GDB was configured as "alpha-redhat-linux".

PING harold.uncarved.co.uk (10.0.1.1): 56 data bytes Program received signal
SIGBUS, Bus error. 0x12000359c in tvsub (out=0x11ffffa30, in=0x1201187fc)
at ping.c:885 885 if ((out->tv_usec -= in->tv_usec) < 0) { (gdb)

In otherwords, gdb has run the program and helpfully stopped at the offending
line.  Now, you just have to figure out how to fix the error...

Sean



