Pawel Skonecki p.304 <stona@kft.umcs.lublin.pl> wrote:
: I have two Alpha servers (Microway) and I would like to set up a claster
: between them. 
: I don't know which type of clasters I can use for my idea.
: I need an advice.
I would say this depends on what you want to do with the cluster.
You will have to have parallel software to run on it. Most of those packages
require a certain parallelizing package like PVM or MPI. You can also run
both PVM and MPI on the same machines at the same time.
More is in fact not needed for a start.
If you have high communication overhead for your parallel application, i.e.
many communications with small packets, you should also think of redesigning
your networking hardware. Fast Ethernet with an active switching HUB is a
nice starting point. But there are other alternatives like for example
Myrinet (though Linux/Alpha is not yet a supported platform for Myrinet
AFAIK).
On the buttomline one could say: The problem is not the "cluster" itself but
rather the parallel application you want to run. What you cannot do is take
a of-the-shelf package and expect it to run faster because you install some
magic cluster software. Your application has to support parallization.

: Pawel Skonecki
