On Mon, 23 Aug 1999, Mike Kuht wrote:

>Continuing this theme ... what if I don't have a Floppy or CD-ROM on the
>Multia but I do have a CD on a Linux Box connected to the network ? Any
>chance of installing over the network using bootp/tftp ?

It can be done, as I did it, but don't take this too lightly.
Here's what I did:

* Review the SRM Firmware Howto from azstarnet.com or alphalinux.org,
specifically about aboot's network booting abilities.

* Set up a second linux box as a bootp/tftp server.

* On another linux/Alpha system, compile a kernel, specifying the root
filesystem on NFS. E-mail me if you need a suitable config file for
DEC's patched 2.0.35 for the Multia. In any case, you'll need the NCR8xx
SCSI driver (not available when doing make menuconfig, you'll need the
make config one), the DE4x5 driver (or Tulip, but I haven't tried), and
'Noname' as the system type. Be sure to compile the DE4x5 driver in the
kernel (you need it for booting ;-).

* On the same system, compile aboot: build the netboot image which
includes the kernel you just built. You need aboot, as Milo can't handle
network boots.

* On the bootp/tftp server, create an NFS-exported directory for use as
the root filesystem by your multia. Fill this directory with at least
/bin/sh, /lib/* etc. (TIP: download some .rpm's from your favourite RH
mirror and install them using the NFS-exported directory as --root. Be
sure to initialise the RPM database in that directory first.)

* If the multia currently using ARC, switch the console to SRM. You'll
need it to boot from the network.

* In SRM, set ewa0_protocols to bootp, set boot_osflags to
"root=/dev/nfs nfsaddrs=bootp
nfsroot=/your/exported/directory/on/your/second/box init=/bin/sh"

* Type boot ewa0 and cross your fingers!

Now, if all goes well, you have a completely diskless box. You can now
go ahead and create filesystems on your SCSI disk, and install the
rest of your system manually.

Hope this helps.

-- 

