Robert McGwier <rwmcgwier@home.com> wrote in message
news:37BF10C6.4807CB33@home.com...
> Wrong again.  I am looking as we speak at the ISO9660 Redhat 6.0 Alpha
disk.
> There
> isn't a file name on it that is as short as 8.3 except for the DOS
utilities
> (making the image,
> ramdisk, milo, etc.).  That is because it uses the 32 bit FAT and long
filenames.
> There is
> also a 8.3 abomination naming convention so that the disk can be read
under DOS.
> Long
> file names are supported.
>
> Bob
>

ISO9660 has no long filenames. The reason that on a Linux CD you have long
filenames (and also case sensitivity) is that it uses Rockridge extensions.
This
is an extension to ISO9660 to support long and case sensitive filenames.
Such
a CD is recognizable by a directory rr_moved. Mounted on Linux, this
directory
is empty. Mounted on MSDOS or Win95, it contains unreachable names in
ISO9660.
Microsoft uses a different approach to get long filenames, called Joliet.
This also
provides support for different languages. Modern Linux kernels are able to
read
Joliet CDs.
This all has nothing to do with FAT32. This is a filesystem for fixed disks
and
is not found on CDs.

Martin Sinot
