On Tue, 17 Aug 1999 14:52:11 GMT, Helick <20vogel@wmich.edu> wrote:
>What doesn't anyone want them?
>I am getting a Multia and I want to see in what king of "trouble" I am
>getting.  :)

The "problem" is that you'll probably need to spend $300 or so to add
in a SCSI disk drive and RAM, and wind up with something that's
unfortunately not nearly as powerful as the used Pentium/166 that you
could get for about that price.

The SCSI "downsides" are three:
a) SCSI II, and nothing newer.
b) You can only fit itty bitty SCSI drives inside a Multia.
c) Many Multias don't have an external SCSI port, meaning you need to
   Roll Your Own
d) IDE is a possibility, but reportedly *kills* performance when disk
   access takes place.

The CPU/Memory downsides are thus:
a) Only 256K of cache, which really isn't enough.
b) Fairly slow memory bus.
c) Expensive "Full Parity" RAM; no EDO.
d) Depending on what you do with the box, it's either "only" as potent
as a P/150 or a P/75.

Graphics downside:
a) Built-in TGA only offers 256 colours and fixed resolution, and
b) There's only one PCI slot, and the only graphics cards I'm aware
people have had success with are Matrox Millennium (*original*
version) and S3 968 2MB (Diamond Stealth Video 3200).

One upside: Built-in 10BaseT and 10Base2 Ethernet.
