This is exactly what I've done for my multia. I have a multia with an
 internal SCSI card with raiser to mount an internal SCSI disk
(i.e. not the one with PCI raiser).

All I've done are:
     - Get an internal SCSI cable with one 50pin male connector, and two
       std 50 pin female connectors.
     - Get a SCSI adapter which converters internal 50pin male connector
        to external 50pin HD female connector (SCSI-2 connector). The
        convertor looks like this
              http://www.tmcscsi.com/adapters/sm003pc.html
        (NOTE: I'm not in a position to recommend who/where to buy it).
        This is just  what I got and I simply remove the PC bracket and put
        the adapter onto my multia.
     - The 50pin male connector hooks up to the SCSI cable that comes
       with the internal SCSI card.
     - One of the 50pin female connector hooks up to the internal SCSI
        drive.
     - Another 50pin female connector hooks up to the SCSI adapter.

Note that there is not a lot of room inside multia to play with. I couldn't
place the SCSI cable on top of the drive, so I have to wrap it below the
SCSI drive raiser and pull it out sideway close to the SCSI card.
I am able to use both the internal SCSI drive and an external CD-ROM
at the same time (this is  how I installed the OS on my multia).

Hope this is useful.

- Kris


