Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

Hi folks!

A few days ago I bought a (terribly inexpensive) 100mbit Ethernet card.
The board features a 8139 chipset by realtek. I plugged the card into my
sx164 and compiled the kernel to contain the rtl8139 driver. This gave
me compile-time error. After fiddling around the source I found out that
the driver would compile as a module. Done it. It worked. For four
"ping" packets. 
I went back to the kernel source and checked for the problem. After
enabling debugging, the driver told me about a problem with "oversized
ethernet frames! status: badfeed"!!! I went back to the source and tried
to find out about what might be ment by "badfeed". It took me about 15
min (or so) to realize that this obscure word actually was a hex-number
(!!) Now I realized that the "oversized ethernet..." might not be the
real problem - since the driver should actually continue working after
getting this error. I even upgraded to kernel version 2.2.11 - didn't
help any. Did anybody (ever) get rtl8139.c to work on alphas?

According to Donald Becker's homepage the driver worked for 2.0.x on
alphas. Why doesn't it work on 2.2.x any more? 

Thanks for your help,

Ciao,


-- 

Sebastian Cramer

