On 11 Aug 1999 10:14:14 +0300, Osmo Kujala <kujala@tukki.cc.jyu.fi> wrote:
> Rick Brice <r.w.NO_SPAM_@_NO_SPAM.lmco.com> wrote:
> > ALSO, the multia does not use True Parity SIMMS!
> > The multia uses ECC SIMMS. 
> 
> I don't believe you. If there exist ECC simms, that are not same as
> True Parity simms, there would be no sense to use them. 21066 prosessor
> does the ECC calculation using 8 parity bits of a pair of 36-bit simms.
> How could simms calculate right ECC bits for Processor? Is ECC simm only
> protecting bits inside itself? No sense.
> 
> > There is a wealth of information on the web about SIMMS
> 
> So where is definition of those special ECC simms? I didn't find any.
> I've been buying simms and dimms many years and allways ECC and true
> parity have been synonymous. Only price may differ.
> Well, I could guess that there have been models of simms, that have more
> than 4 extra bits for ECC. Then ECC could be calculated for one simm too?

Basically, there are three different kinds of ECC, which might cause some
confusion:

   (1) Using parity SIMMs; two x36 parity SIMMS supply a total of 72 bits.
       64 bits are used for data and 8 bits for ECC.
       The SIMMS are standard true parity SIMMS; the MB/CPU has to support
       the ECC functionality.
   (2) Using ECC SIMMs; these are special SIMMs (39 or 40 bits).
       The ECC is done inside the SIMM itself. The MB must specifically
       support ECC SIMMS.
   (3) EOS (ECC on SIMM) SIMMs.
       The ECC is done inside the SIMM. To the MB the SIMM acts as a
       normal parity SIMM.

I'm not sure if the Multia does ECC on the MB or not, but I am _positive_ that
the Multia requires _true parity_ SIMMs and will _not work_ with ECC SIMMs.


Greetings,

Ton.
