On Wed, 11 Aug 1999, Ton Nijkes wrote:

>   (1) Using parity SIMMs; two x36 parity SIMMS supply a total of 72 bits.
>       64 bits are used for data and 8 bits for ECC.
>       The SIMMS are standard true parity SIMMS; the MB/CPU has to support
>       the ECC functionality.

[SNIP]

>I'm not sure if the Multia does ECC on the MB or not, but I am _positive_ that
>the Multia requires _true parity_ SIMMs and will _not work_ with ECC SIMMs.

The memory controller contained in the 21066(A) Alpha chip does the ECC
work. And you're absolutely right; there is a 72-bit memory path between
the CPU and the memory in the Multia. 

The best thing to ask for when shopping for SIMMs is 36-bit, fast page
mode (no EDO) ones, so the vendor won't be confused by the different
parity / ECC variants.

