As far as I know, there is no such kind of boot disk as in i386. So there
is not such a  tool either.
In my thought, you do not need such a boot disk.
If your file system and partition table are not in a mess, generally you
can use the boot floppy ( made from generic.img or the kernel you built)
to boot the system and specify the appropriate hard disk partition as
root.
Otherwise, I think you have to install Linux again.

create method: find the kernel image you use to boot. It should be in
/boot or / and be named as vmlinux.gz or vmlinuz.

$ mke2fs   /dev/fd0
 $ e2writeboot   /boot/bootlx  ( If you use milo, you don't need this
step).
 $ mount /dev/fd0   /mnt
 $ cp  vmlinux.gz ( the path of kernel  image)    /mnt
 $umount /mnt

