Greg, Eric, you are both missing the big picture.  There is only so
much effort that is being put into Linux development.  So at issue is
what things can be done to result in the best int_time(gain*usage),
not gain in some SPEC programs right now only.

Look, do you want to guess what most speed-sensitive-Linux users
really care about right now?  They want to know things like why the
heck Netscape thrashes the disk when there should be plenty of memory
if a really good disk caching algorithm was being used, what the
number of transactions their web server can process per second is, why
commercial Linux binaries are so big, and why the guy in a basement in
Idaho who changed 10 lines of code so that the migraine-inducing
hdparm interface to the kernel would work with his laptop's hard drive
never emailed a patch to Redhat.  There are currently severe
weaknesses in Linux that really slow down many people's lives.  Lack
of page coloring isn't causing much of the pain.

Sure, people look at SPEC tables.  Heck, I'm a Fortran-speed freak
myself with ``tons'' of legacy Fortran/FWEB/Ratfor/code.  And I'd love
it if companies submitted results from machines using the Linux OS.
But right now there are relatively easy things that can (and need to)
be done to make performance in out-of-the-box Linux drastically
better.  Worry about this corner of the frosting only after the cake
batter at least has less bitterness to it.

Jason
