Daniel Keren writes:

> By the way, I am interested mainly in floating point (double precision)
> calculations, and my programs are written in C or C++.

I am interested in this topic also, especially multiprocessor alpha
systems (I want to port algorithms for digital hearing aids which were
implemented on signal processor systems until now). I found it
informative to look at the high performance page at alphalinux.org:

http://www.alphalinux.org/docs/high_perf.shtml


torvalds@transmeta.com (Linus Torvalds) writes:

> 
> And I know that Fortran people think they are special. But the fact is
> that 99% of the worlds computing power (the part that isn't sitting
> idle, that is), is used for other things. There's a hell of a lot more
> floating point being done on games than on atomic simulations, I bet.
> 


In the scientific area it seems to be impossible to get rid of Fortran
for number-crunching tasks.  In the above mentioned page, there is a
contribution (http://www.hpcf.cam.ac.uk/C_rant.html) which tries to
explain why C compilers never will be able to generate as efficient
code as Fortran compilers. It seems to me that in practice the most
important point for high performance numerical tasks are the
compilers.

Besides, it seems one could try to take into account the cache
architecture when implementing algorithms, as is explained in the same
location - I don't know if this remains true?

However if one follows the rule that 5% of the code use 95% of the CPU
time, one should be able to get an quite large advantage from the use
of special assembled libraries (I am not aware if they are available
for Linux, but it would be great as number-crunching and games are
getting closer steadily).


