> I read in the FAQ : "You must use BSD-style >
> disklabels and not the
> fdisk linux command."

A bit misinformation linux fdisk supports
BSD-disklabels..
In install, use the fdisk command.
Just remove all the "ordinary linux" partitions
and then give command B.
It get you to the BSD-style disklabeling.
Create one big C partition beginning at
10 cylinders. ('n <enter> c <enter>')
Then add for example 2 or three other partitions (
a b ..)
Set the partition types. ('t')

Then write the table to the disk ('w'), exit fdisk
and continue with install.

REMEMBER! DO NOT use the first 'menu' in fdisk.
Proceed always straight to the bsd-labeling with
'b <enter>' !!!!!!!!!!!!!1

> Please tell me what is " BSD-style disklabels"
> and how to partition my
> hard disk before lanching the swriteboot
> commande.

After install or using the console during install
give command:

# swriteboot -f1 -f2 -f3 /dev/sda1 -v

-f1 = a partition
-f2 = b partition
-f3 = c partition
-f4 = d partition
-f5 = e partition
-f6 = f partition
-f7 = g partition
-f8 = h partition

This worked for me..in two different
Alphas..Alphaserver 4100 and one
Alphaworkstation..

Dunno if it's bug or if I did something wrong
but I had to copy (during install) the vmlinux.gz
from the initial boot floppy to /boot/ directory
to get it boot..

At srm use this command to boot:

>>> boot dkc100 -file 1/boot/vmlinux.gz -fl
"root=/dev/sda1"

where 1 is the partition a in my configs..

If any questions please use email.

